--TEST--
`await` consumes the started isolate a handle names, so a handle awaited twice throws rather than waiting again — ADR 0006 § *Decision*
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
echo "from the child";
return 7;
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The handle is a `Core\Script\Handle`, whose one slot holds the key its
// running isolate was filed under on this request
// (`nvs_runtime::Ctx::hold_started_script`). `await` takes that entry, not the
// object, so the parent still owns `$job` afterwards — and there is nothing
// left for a second `await` to collect.
var $job = spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
var $first = await $job;
echo "ok=", $first->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo "output=", $first->output, "\n";

try {
    var $again = await $job;
    echo "unreachable", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $twice) {
    echo $twice->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
ok=true
output=from the child
`await` was given a handle a previous `await` already collected
